# herdr-pr-tracker — configuration.
#
#   cp config.example config
#
# `config` is gitignored: it is yours, and this file is what ships. Two
# locations are read — this one (the repo root, handy while the plugin is linked
# live) and then the plugin config dir, which is read second and so wins:
#
#   herdr plugin config-dir herdr-pr-tracker
#
# Everything is commented out at its default. The pane reads its config once at
# startup, so toggle the widget off and on after editing — including to clear
# the warning below.
#
# `KEY=value`, one per line, `#` comments a line out. A value may be quoted or
# bare; a matched pair of single or double quotes is stripped.
#
# Glyphs, colours, signal precedence and sort order are deliberately NOT
# configurable. A widget whose meaning depends on settings is a widget you have
# to remember the settings of before you can read it.

# Seconds between GitHub polls. Rate-limit cost is charged per `search` field
# rather than per pull request, so a poll costs the same whatever comes back —
# a rounding error against the 5000/hour GraphQL budget at any sane interval.
# The exact point count is deliberately not quoted here: read `rateLimit.cost`
# off a live response if it matters. The limit is your attention, not the API.
#POLL_SECONDS=60

# The GitHub search. This is the one knob that changes *what* is tracked, and it
# re-aims the **authored** view entirely — narrow it to one org, say, with
# `user:acme`. `archived:false` keeps unmergeable PRs in archived repos out.
#
# Do NOT point it at `review-requested:@me` to watch other people's PRs: that is
# what the second view is for (toggle it from the pane header), and it is not
# the same thing. The inbound view leads its rows with the author, ranks the
# signals for a reviewer rather than an author, and marks the pull requests
# nobody actually asked you about. Routed through here those rows would be
# rendered as though they were yours.
#
# This setting deliberately does not reach the inbound view at all.
#SEARCH_QUERY="is:pr is:open author:@me archived:false"

# Repositories and owners whose pull requests are never fetched, at all, in
# either view. Separate entries with spaces or commas. Duplicates collapse, and
# they collapse case-insensitively: GitHub's qualifiers ignore case, so
# `Acme/Web-App` and `acme/web-app` are one ignore and count once in the warning
# below.
#
#   IGNORE_REPOS="acme/web-app acme/metrics-service"
#
# The slash carries the meaning, and it is not optional:
#
#   acme/web-app   one repository
#   acme/          every repository under one owner
#   web-app        an ERROR — dropped (see below)
#
# A dropped entry shows at the foot of the list as `⚠ 2 bad IGNORE_REPOS
# entries` — `⚠ 1 bad IGNORE_REPOS entry` for one — the count only, because the
# pane is narrow. To see *which* entries, run `bun bin/pane.ts` by hand: it
# names them on stderr before it takes the screen. Inside a Herdr pane that line
# is painted and immediately covered, so the count is the signal you get.
#
# The warning is computed at startup along with the rest of this file, so fixing
# an entry clears it at the next restart rather than at the next poll. Its line
# is reserved out of the pane's height and cannot be crowded out by a long list;
# its width is not reserved, so a very narrow pane truncates the message and one
# narrower still drops the line rather than wrap it over the header.
#
# A bare name looks like the obvious thing to type and is exactly what must not
# be guessed at: GitHub answers a repository qualifier with no owner by
# subtracting nothing and reporting no error, so a parser that guessed would
# hand you a filter that silently does not filter. There is no owner wildcard
# to fall back on.
#
# Unlike SEARCH_QUERY this **does** reach the inbound view, because it can only
# remove rows — it cannot relabel a row that stays, which is the thing the
# inbound searches are protected from.
#
# Two consequences of subtracting at the search rather than hiding at the paint.
# Ignored pull requests cost no page budget, so a noisy repository stops
# crowding others out of MAX_PRS. And the pane never learns what it dropped, so
# there is no "N ignored" marker, and `0 open · ✓ all clear` is a claim about
# what you track rather than about GitHub — which is already true of
# SEARCH_QUERY. A typo in an otherwise well-formed entry cannot be detected
# either: it subtracts nothing, and looks exactly like an entry that works.
#
# Each entry adds a negated qualifier to every search, so the query grows with
# the list. Forty negations were probed against the live API and accepted; no
# ceiling above that has been measured. There is no cap enforced here, because a
# cap has to drop entries to enforce itself, and a silently dropped ignore is the
# failure this whole setting is shaped against. If a list ever does grow past
# what GitHub will take, that arrives as a failed refresh in the pane header —
# loud, and not something you could mistake for a filter that is working.
#IGNORE_REPOS=""

# Maximum PRs fetched in one request (GitHub's search page cap is 100). PRs
# beyond this are counted in the "+N older" row rather than silently dropped.
#MAX_PRS=100

# auto | always | never. `auto` shows the owner only when a PR's owner differs
# from the one most of your PRs share, so the common case stays compact.
#SHOW_OWNER=auto

# Set to 0/off/false/no to render without colour, leaving the glyphs to carry
# the state. `COLOUR` is accepted as well, and `COLOR` wins if both are set.
#COLOR=on

# Minimum seconds between sidebar-token lookups for a single pane. Focus events
# fire far more often than a PR's state changes, and each lookup is two `gh`
# subprocesses.
#TOKEN_THROTTLE_SECONDS=30
